      "Longer expandable hoses are harder to build well, because the tube must hold pressure across more length and the expansion and contraction cycle stresses the fittings more. Hospaip uses a triple latex core in its longer lengths specifically to handle that, and in our testing the 100-foot version expanded reliably to close to its rated length and survived a season without a burst, which several competitors at similar lengths did not manage. The practical appeal is real. A 100-foot conventional hose is a heavy, awkward thing to move and store, while the same reach in an expandable contracts to a bundle you can carry under one arm. For a property where the spigot is far from the far end of the garden, that difference in handling is significant. Solid brass connectors, a shut-off valve, and a ten-pattern nozzle round out a well-specified package at a reasonable per-foot price. The physics catch up at length, though. Flow rate through a narrow expandable core over 100 feet is meaningfully reduced, and it shows most when running a sprinkler rather than a hand nozzle. Full expansion takes noticeably longer than a shorter hose at the same pressure, and full drainage before storage takes real patience, which matters because incomplete drainage before a freeze is a reliable way to destroy a latex hose.",
