  {
    id: "zero-g-alternative",
    name: "Zero-G Lightweight Garden Hose",
    category: "Non-Expandable Alternative, Woven Composite, 5/8\"",
    badge: "Best Alternative",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 22600,
    pros: [
      "Full 5/8-inch bore delivers much better flow than any expandable hose",
      "Roughly half the weight of a rubber hose while being far more durable than latex",
      "Not vulnerable to the burst failure mode that defines the expandable category",
      "Highly kink resistant and coils into a compact bundle without expanding or contracting",
      "Tolerates being left pressurized and handles sun exposure far better than latex",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not contract, so it needs more storage space than an expandable hose",
      "Heavier than an expandable hose of the same length",
      "Costs more than most expandable hoses",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If what you actually want is light and compact rather than literally expandable, this solves the same problem without the burst risk.",
    description:
      "It is worth including one honest alternative, because a substantial number of people buying expandable hoses do not specifically want expansion. They want a hose that is light, easy to handle, does not kink, and stores compactly, and expandables are simply the most heavily marketed answer to that. The Zero-G delivers all four without the latex tube that creates the burst risk. Its woven composite construction over a full 5/8-inch inner bore keeps weight to roughly half that of a comparable rubber hose while delivering flow that no expandable can match, which is immediately obvious when running a sprinkler. It is highly kink resistant, coils into a compact bundle, and it can be left pressurized or in the sun without the anxiety that a latex hose deserves. Over a season of testing it showed no degradation at all beyond cosmetic jacket dirt. The trade-offs are honest. It does not contract, so a 50-foot Zero-G occupies more storage space than a 50-foot expandable contracted to 17 feet, and it weighs more. It also costs more than most expandable hoses. If storage space is genuinely the binding constraint, an expandable still wins. If what you wanted was light and kink-free with good flow, this is the better tool and it will not burst on a hot afternoon.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
