          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Expandable Garden Hoses",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-expandable-garden-hose/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best expandable garden hose?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Flexi Hose is our overall pick, combining a double latex core with solid brass fittings and a heavy woven jacket, and it survived a full season of testing intact. For maximum durability, TheFitLife triple-layer latex hose was the toughest we tested, and for a budget option that does not cut the corners that matter, GrowGreen offers double latex and brass at a lower price.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do expandable hoses last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A cheap single-layer hose used carelessly often fails within a season. A double or triple-layer latex hose with brass fittings, depressurized after each use and stored in shade and drained before winter, commonly lasts two to four years. Care habits affect lifespan more than any other factor in this category.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do expandable hoses burst?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most bursts come from leaving the hose pressurized, which keeps the latex tube stretched to its limit indefinitely, especially in sun where trapped water heats and pressure rises further. UV degradation, freezing with water inside, and abrasion that wears through the protective fabric jacket account for nearly all the rest.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do expandable hoses have good water pressure?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Flow through an expandable hose is lower than through a conventional 5/8-inch hose because the latex core has a narrower bore. That is fine for hand watering with a nozzle and noticeably limiting for oscillating sprinklers or filling large containers. If you run sprinklers regularly, a conventional hose is the better tool.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do expandable hoses work with low water pressure?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They need adequate pressure to expand fully, generally around 40 PSI or more. On a spigot with weak flow, an expandable hose may only reach 70 to 80 percent of its rated length. If your pressure is low, buy a longer hose than your measurement suggests and test the actual expansion before relying on it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you leave an expandable hose outside in winter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Water expanding as it freezes splits the latex tube permanently, and expandable hoses retain water in the contracted fabric folds even when they look drained. Disconnect, stretch the hose out downhill, drain it fully with both ends open, and store it indoors before the first freeze.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
