    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Expandable hoses are the most polarizing product in the garden aisle. They weigh a quarter of what a rubber hose weighs, coil into nothing, and never kink, which is genuinely useful. They also have a reputation for bursting within a season, which is genuinely earned. Both things are true, and the difference between a hose that lasts three months and one that lasts three years is mostly about construction and care. Here is what to buy and how to keep it alive.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: How Expandable Hoses Actually Work -->
        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Expandable Hoses Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Understanding the construction explains every strength and every weakness of the category, and it makes the care instructions obvious rather than arbitrary.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The inner tube is latex.</strong> A thin, highly elastic latex tube runs the length of the hose. When water pressure enters, the tube stretches both in diameter and in length, typically expanding to roughly three times its contracted length. When pressure is released, the latex contracts back. That elasticity is the entire mechanism.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The outer jacket is woven fabric.</strong> A loose woven polyester sleeve surrounds the latex tube. When contracted, the fabric bunches up; when the latex expands, the fabric straightens out and reaches its full length, at which point it physically limits further expansion and protects the latex from abrasion and sunlight. Jacket density, measured in denier, is a direct predictor of how long the hose survives being dragged around.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The fittings are the stress point.</strong> Every expansion and contraction cycle pulls on the connection between the elastic tube, the fabric jacket, and the rigid coupling. This junction sees more mechanical stress than anything else on the hose, which is why plastic fittings crack and why solid brass is worth insisting on.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water pressure determines expansion.</strong> A hose rated to 50 feet reaches that length only at adequate pressure. On a spigot with low flow, an expandable hose may only reach 70 or 80 percent of its rated length, which surprises buyers who measured their yard carefully. Most need somewhere in the range of 40 PSI or more to expand fully.
          </p>

