        <!-- SECTION 2: Why Expandable Hoses Fail -->
        <div id="why-they-fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Expandable Hoses Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            The category has a genuinely bad reputation, and it is largely deserved for cheap hoses used carelessly. Here is exactly what goes wrong, because every one of these failures is preventable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Left Pressurized</h3>

          <p>
            This is the single biggest killer. Leaving the spigot on with the nozzle closed keeps the latex tube stretched to its limit indefinitely, and if the hose is in sunlight the trapped water heats and pressure rises further. Latex left under sustained tension weakens and eventually splits. A conventional hose tolerates this; an expandable one does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sun Exposure</h3>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light degrades latex quickly. The fabric jacket provides some protection, but a hose left lying on a sunny driveway all summer will fail far sooner than one stored in shade. This is why the hoses that last are the ones stored in a shed, a hose pot, or under an eave.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Freezing With Water Inside</h3>

          <p>
            Water expanding as it freezes splits a latex tube instantly and permanently. Expandable hoses hold water in the contracted fabric folds even when they appear drained, so proper draining means disconnecting, stretching the hose out downhill, and letting it run fully empty before storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Abrasion</h3>

          <p>
            The fabric jacket is the only thing protecting the latex, and dragging it across rough concrete, gravel, or a splintery deck wears through threads. Once the jacket is compromised, the latex has no protection and fails quickly. This is the main reason expandables suit lawns and beds better than driveways and workshops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plastic Fittings</h3>

          <p>
            Every expansion cycle stresses the junction between the elastic tube and the rigid coupling. Plastic fittings crack there, then leak, then separate. This failure is entirely a function of buying a cheap hose, and it is why solid brass is the specification worth paying for.
          </p>

